WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.116
Client-supplied file names should be treated as display metadata, not as storage decisions.

2
00:00:06.116 --> 00:00:19.827
A file name may be useful to show a user which invoice, report, or image they selected, but it should not decide the server path, object key, final storage location, access policy, or processing behavior.

3
00:00:19.827 --> 00:00:23.591
Generate safe internal names or identifiers for storage.

4
00:00:23.591 --> 00:00:33.941
The application can preserve a sanitized display name separately when the business needs it, while using a server-generated identifier for actual storage.

5
00:00:33.941 --> 00:00:42.342
This separates user-facing labels from operational storage details and reduces accidental reliance on client-provided values.

6
00:00:42.342 --> 00:00:44.560
Do not use client-supplied paths.

7
00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:57.532
Upload workflows should avoid accepting paths, folder decisions, object prefixes, or storage locations from the caller unless those values are mapped through a tightly controlled business rule.

8
00:00:57.532 --> 00:01:04.051
The server should decide where temporary, quarantined, private, public, and processed files live.

9
00:01:04.051 --> 00:01:08.688
Uploaded files should be stored outside executable application roots.

10
00:01:08.688 --> 00:01:19.644
A file accepted from a user should not become part of the application code path, static content root, or executable deployment area merely because it was uploaded.

11
00:01:19.644 --> 00:01:25.692
Storage design should prevent uploaded content from being treated as application behavior.

12
00:01:25.692 --> 00:01:29.255
Storage areas should reflect file state and audience.

13
00:01:29.255 --> 00:01:43.369
Temporary files, quarantined files, private user files, public published files, processed derivatives, thumbnails, and rejected uploads may need separate locations, policies, cleanup schedules, and permissions.

14
00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:48.141
Clear state separation makes the workflow easier to review and operate.

15
00:01:48.141 --> 00:01:51.098
Apply least privilege to storage identities.

16
00:01:51.098 --> 00:02:00.104
The web application, background scanner, processing worker, support tool, and administrator account may not need the same permissions.

17
00:02:00.104 --> 00:02:11.261
Limit read, write, delete, publish, and listing permissions according to each component's job, and review those permissions during release and infrastructure changes.
